==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[wheel house]]
* [[wheel-house]]

===Etymology===
{{compound|wheel|house|lang=en}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈʍiːlˌhaʊs/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} An [[enclosed]] [[compartment]], on the [[deck]] of a [[vessel]] such as a [[fishing boat]], from which it may be [[navigate]]d; on a larger vessel it is the [[bridge]] or [[pilothouse]].
#: {{syn|en|bridge|pilothouse}}
# {{lb|en|automotive}} The partially enclosed structure above and around a wheel of an [[automobile]], typically partly formed by a portion of a [[fender]] panel that has been extended outward beyond the plane of the rest of the panel.
#: {{syn|en|wheel arch}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[enclosed]] structure around side paddlewheels on a steamboat.
#: {{syn|en|paddle box}}
# {{lb|en|archaeology}} A [[prehistoric]] [[structure]] from the [[Iron Age]], found in [[Scotland]], characteristically including an outer wall within which a circle of stone piers (bearing a resemblance to the spokes of a wheel) form the basis for lintel arches supporting corbelled roofing with a [[hearth]] at the hub.
# {{lb|en|baseball|US}} A [[pitch]] [[location]] which is favorable to the [[hitter]].
#: {{ux|en|The pitch was right in his '''wheelhouse''', and he hit a grand slam.}}
# {{lb|en|slang|US}} A person's [[expertise]] or area of authority.
#: {{ux|en|Horse viruses are in Pat's '''wheelhouse'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Employee bonuses are completely in Sam's '''wheelhouse'''.}}

